.TH WEBFS 4
.SH NAME
webfs \- HTTP client file server, one browsing session
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B webfs
[
.B -D
]
[
.I mountpoint
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Webfs
serves HTTP and HTTPS as files, by default at
.BR /mnt/web .
Each request has its own directory, allocated by reading
.BR clone ;
requests run concurrently, so a slow one delays only the readers of
its own files.
All requests share one cookie jar: a
.I webfs
is a browsing session, and a different session is a different
.IR webfs ,
mounted elsewhere or in another name space.
.PP
The root holds:
.TF cookies
.PD
.TP
.B clone
Reading it allocates a connection directory and returns its number.
.TP
.B ctl
Global settings; reading gives them, writing
.BI useragent " string"
sets the default
.B User-Agent
header.
.TP
.B cookies
The cookie jar (RFC 6265), one cookie per line:
.IP
.EX
domain path name=value expires secure httponly hostonly
.EE
.IP
.I Expires
is in seconds since the epoch, 0 for a session cookie; the flags are 0 or 1.
Writing lines in the same form adds or replaces cookies; writing
.B clear
empties the jar.
Responses' Set-Cookie headers fill it, and requests send the cookies
that match them, across redirects as well.
.PP
A connection directory
.I N
holds:
.TF contenttype
.PD
.TP
.B ctl
Written before the request is made:
.BI url " url"\f1,
.BI method " method"
(GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, HEAD, PATCH or OPTIONS),
.BI header " Name: value"
and
.B "cookies off"
(the request neither sends the jar's cookies nor stores those its
response sets: a script's cross-origin request without credentials) or
.BR "cookies on" .
Reading gives what has been set.
.TP
.B postbody
The request body.
.TP
.B body
The response body.
Opening it for reading, or reading
.BR status ,
.BR contenttype ,
.B url
or
.BR header ,
makes the request; each waits until it completes.
A body sent with a gzip or deflate Content-Encoding is decoded.
.TP
.B status
The status code and line, or
.BI error: " message"\f1.
.TP
.B contenttype
The response's Content-Type.
.TP
.B url
The URL that answered, after any redirects; relative references in the
body are relative to it.
.TP
.B header
The response header, one
.B Name: value
per line.
.TP
.B parsed/
The request URL's parts:
.BR url ,
.BR scheme ,
.BR host ,
.BR port ,
.BR path ,
.BR query ,
.BR fragment .
.PP
Unless the request sets its own,
.I webfs
sends
.B "Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate"
and its default User-Agent.
Changing the request
.RB ( ctl
or
.BR postbody )
after it was made makes it again on the next read.
.PP
Connections stay until they are no longer open and 32 newer idle
connections exist, so a client may write
.B ctl
and read
.B body
with separate opens.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
; webfs
; c=`{cat /mnt/web/clone}
; echo url https://example.org/ >/mnt/web/$c/ctl
; cat /mnt/web/$c/status
200 HTTP/1.1 200 OK
; cat /mnt/web/$c/body
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/webfs.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/webclient.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR web9p (4),
.IR charon (1)
.SH BUGS
There is no HTTP cache yet.
.br
A response body is held in memory, up to 64 megabytes.
